From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mempage4k <mempage4k@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 19:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] wgpu-core: a pass's barriers first, in the open command buffer

Where command buffers cannot be suspended (Vulkan, since wgpu-hal 0005
left it out), each render pass ended the command buffer before it and took
two of its own: one for the pass, one for the barriers before it (recorded
after the pass, because only then are its resources known). What came
after the pass (copies, clears) began yet another. On a Pixel 8a (Mali)
that cost ~16 ms of CPU a frame (~54 passes): 34 -> 50 ms of encoding,
29 -> 25 fps.

A render pass's resources are known before it is recorded when nothing
but its attachments and the bind groups, vertex and index buffers its
commands set adds a usage: no queries, timestamps, bundles or indirect
draws, and no discarded surface to clear first. Such a pass now merges
those into a usage scope, records its barriers just before the render
pass begins (a hook in RenderPassInfo::start), and goes on in the command
buffer the encoder has open, which stays open after it. Nothing is
applied after the pass: the tracker already holds its usages, and
applying them again would add barriers (a write after a write needs one
where usages are not ordered, as on Vulkan) out of order.

And a pass that needs nothing before it (no barriers, clears, query
resets or indirect draw validation) takes no command buffer for it.

Measured on Metal with the tracker doing the same work (its barriers
record nothing there): 93-94 % of a game's render passes take the first
path (95 % on a Pixel 8a), and in none of them did the pass find a
barrier after it that the scan had missed. Metal keeps suspending
command buffers (0007), where barriers go at the end of the one before.
---
